# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored.
OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://your-llm-endpoint.example.com
OPENAI_MODEL=gemini-3.5-flash
OPENAI_API_KEY=your-key-here
# Global default reasoning effort (a per-agent frontmatter `reasoning:` or a per-message value
# overrides it). low|medium|high|xhigh; empty = the endpoint's own default. Endpoint-dependent.
# OPENAI_REASONING_EFFORT=medium

# Retrieval over the documents in a workspace's mnt/knowledge/ folder (optional). The endpoint and
# the key fall back to OPENAI_* above, because one gateway usually serves both — set nothing here and
# it just works, provided that gateway answers /v1/embeddings.
#
# The MODEL deliberately does NOT inherit. OPENAI_MODEL names a CHAT model, and a chat model id is
# not an embedding model id. Unset means "auto", which lets the gateway choose — convenient to try,
# wrong to index with: "auto" can resolve to a different model tomorrow, and vectors from two models
# are not comparable, so the whole index silently stops meaning anything. Pin a name before you index
# anything you care about.
#
# DIMS defaults to 768. Providers that offer more (gemini-embedding-001 gives 3072 natively) offer it
# for corpora far larger than one person's documents, and it is paid in every byte of the index and
# every comparison. Only set it if the model refuses to shorten — the adapter checks what came back
# and says so rather than indexing a length nobody chose.
#
# !! CHANGING MODEL OR DIMS INVALIDATES AN EXISTING INDEX. !!
# A vector only means anything relative to the model that produced it. Search a 768-dimension index
# with 1536-dimension query vectors and there is nothing to compare; search a gemini index with
# llama vectors and the arithmetic still works, which is worse — it returns confident nonsense
# instead of an error. So after changing either value:
#
#     rm nocturn-data/workspaces/<name>/knowledge.idx.json
#     nocturn knowledge index -w <name>
#
# The index records the model and dimensions it was built with and refuses to answer when they no
# longer match, so this is a message rather than bad results — but it still has to be rebuilt, and
# rebuilding re-embeds every document, which costs whatever your provider charges.
# NOCTURN_EMBED_BASE_URL=https://your-embeddings-endpoint.example
# NOCTURN_EMBED_API_KEY=your-key-here
# NOCTURN_EMBED_MODEL=gemini-embedding-001
# NOCTURN_EMBED_DIMS=768

# Spoken sessions (optional, experimental): a SECOND model, one that takes and returns a continuous
# audio stream rather than answering a turn at a time. Leave unset and everything else works; a
# device asking for a spoken session is simply told this daemon has none.
#
# As of August 2026 the id below is the ONLY one that works, and the newer ones are the problem:
# they lack asynchronous function calling, so the conversation stalls the moment the assistant uses
# a tool — it waits for the call to return before saying anything. That is unusable rather than
# slow, because a tool call is exactly where an approval happens: "let me check", then silence for
# as long as your phone takes to answer. Do not bump this to a newer id until one of them gains
# asynchronous function calling.
#
# There is still no default baked into the binary. These ids change often, and the list a key can
# actually reach is not the list in anybody's documentation — check your own account.
# GEMINI_API_KEY=your-key-here
# GEMINI_LIVE_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash-native-audio-latest

# Speaker recognition (optional, experimental): the path to a WeSpeaker ResNet34 ONNX checkpoint
# (~26 MB, never committed — *.onnx is gitignored). Unset means whoami always reports an unknown
# speaker, and the daemon says so at startup so that reads as configuration and not as failure.
# Enrol with: nocturn voices add --device <name> <person> <recordings…>
# NOCTURN_SPEAKER_MODEL=/path/to/wespeaker_en_voxceleb_resnet34.onnx

# Recording the uplink (optional): a directory the daemon writes a device's microphone audio to, as
# 16 kHz mono WAV — the format `nocturn voices add` expects. It exists to capture a voice through
# the microphone, room and echo canceller that will later have to recognise it, which a laptop
# microphone cannot stand in for. OFF unless set, because it writes what the room said to disk;
# arming it logs a warning and every file is logged by name.
# NOCTURN_VOICE_CAPTURE=/path/to/recordings

# Out-of-band approvals (optional): wake a paired phone about an UNATTENDED run's Ask via
# native mobile push (Apple APNs). Leave these empty to keep everything on-LAN over the
# WebSocket. The push is only a WAKE — the approve/deny happens in-app over the authenticated
# WebSocket. Set ALL of KEY/KEY_ID/TEAM_ID/BUNDLE_ID to enable.
# NOCTURN_APNS_KEY=/path/to/AuthKey_XXXXXXXXXX.p8   # token-signing key (.p8, PKCS#8 EC)
# NOCTURN_APNS_KEY_ID=XXXXXXXXXX                     # the key's Key ID
# NOCTURN_APNS_TEAM_ID=YYYYYYYYYY                    # your Apple Developer Team ID
# NOCTURN_APNS_BUNDLE_ID=me.itexpert.nocturn         # the app's bundle id (the apns-topic)
# NOCTURN_APNS_PRODUCTION=1                          # 1 = live APNs host, unset = sandbox

# Developing the app against a running daemon (optional). `ng serve` puts the page on
# http://localhost:4200, which is a DIFFERENT origin from the daemon — so the daemon refuses it, and
# that refusal is doing its job: the thing an attacker's page would read is the RESPONSE, and the
# response to /pair is a bearer. Name the origin explicitly to allow it. Comma-separated.
#
# Do NOT set this on a daemon anyone else can reach.
# NOCTURN_DEV_ORIGINS=http://localhost:4200

# The library: the curated catalog of skills and MCP servers the app browses and installs from.
# Unset — the default — the daemon uses the one this project publishes,
# https://efuturetoday.github.io/nocturn/catalog.json, generated from catalog/ in this repository.
# Nothing is fetched until somebody opens the library.
#
# Point it at your own catalog, or switch the library off entirely:
# NOCTURN_CATALOG_URL=./my-catalog.json
# NOCTURN_CATALOG_URL=off
#
# https:// for a remote host, or a PATH to a file on this machine — a household with its own skills
# should not have to run a web server for them. The catalog carries each skill's whole SKILL.md
# inline, so installing never fetches from a second place, plus a sha256 per entry checked before
# anything is written. That digest is not a signature — whoever serves the catalog serves it too — it
# turns a truncated response into a refusal instead of a half-installed skill. A PLUGIN entry also
# carries an Ed25519 signature, checked against a key compiled into the binary: text is held to the
# channel, code to a key. A catalog read from a file needs none — there is no channel to authenticate.

# Secret vault (optional): host-owned credentials injected into outgoing requests at the border,
# so the model (and any script/plugin) never sees the bearer. Set the master passphrase to unlock
# (or, on first run, initialize) the encrypted vault at ./nocturn-data/vault.enc. Unset = no vault,
# no injection. An interactive unlock prompt replaces this env in a later slice.
# NOCTURN_MASTER_PASSPHRASE=correct-horse-battery-staple
# Seed credential VALUES (until an interactive add-secret UX exists): NOCTURN_SECRET_<NAME>=value is
# stored in the vault under <name> (lowercased). e.g. NOCTURN_SECRET_GITHUB → secret "github".
# NOCTURN_SECRET_GITHUB=ghp_xxx
# Bind a stored secret to a host in ./nocturn-data/bindings.json (gitignored), e.g.:
#   [{"secret":"github","host":"api.github.com","header":"Authorization","prefix":"Bearer "}]
